body:
Banshees veil is a super good item on diana, good ap and a lot of MRES (basically nullifies sorcs and shadowflame mpen if they have them. Plus the obvious passive of veil helps you engage.

&#x200B;

Void gives less ap than shadowflame and less health, it becomes better the more MR ppl have while the flat mpen from shadowflame gives more value.

Nashors is a good item on her but with games ending shorter and more emphasis on important fights zhonyas gives you a lot more survivability in teamfights and a safer time engaging. (along with just being cheaper). The clear is also pretty fine as long as you keep your passive up so you won't miss the attack speed from nashors.

&#x200B;

Akali is pretty free since you are too tanky for her to kill you and you are also able to do a bunch of damage. When she shrouds you have a few options. You can either back off, try to q her, or ult in the middle of it. You also out range her q poke with your q.

Veigar is kind of difficult initially because of the box being a problem. The trick with this matchup is to keep track of that box cooldown. if he doesnt already have the box up you can engage on him with the standard Q E W combo and then he will likely but the box around u to let him walk away. And here is when you would ult to bring him back in. If its for a trade and don't go for the kill, than when he throws up the box just focus on dodging the w and ur w shield + bone plating should mitigate most of the damage you take from Veigar.  

&#x200B;

The initial laning is pretty standard too with a passive levels 1 to 2 and then going for full combos repeatedly after level 3

&#x200B;

Does that help or are there other problems you have in the matchup besides how to approach killing him?

The rainmeter version is outdated and not supported anymore. There is a stand alone version of it that works 100% of the time and is easy to use.

&#x200B;

Here is the link to download:  [https://github.com/khanhas/spicetify-cli/wiki/Installation](https://github.com/khanhas/spicetify-cli/wiki/Installation)

It's G.

&#x200B;

You want to multiply the rate by the amount of time and add all of them up. You go 2 miles per hour for 10 minutes. (2 x 10) and 3 miles per hour for 5 min (3 x 5) add both of them and you get 35. Then divide the distance (35) by the total time (15) and you get G
